  <tr height="1">
    <td  background="images/riga.gif"></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
	<div align=justify><font class="storytext"><P><STRONG>EDGAR BRONFMAN</STRONG>, head of <STRONG>Warner Music</STRONG>, has begun shopping for music management companies as he prepares the business for a possible flotation. <BR><STRONG>Sources close to Bronfman say he wants to expand revenues beyond music sales and add more publishing, merchandising and music management to the business mix.</STRONG> <BR><BR><STRONG>Bronfman</STRONG> bought <STRONG>Warner Music</STRONG> from <STRONG>Time Warner</STRONG> last November for $2.6 billion, beating Britain’s <STRONG>EMI</STRONG>. <STRONG>Warner</STRONG>’s artists include <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>, <STRONG>Led Zeppelin</STRONG> and <STRONG>REM</STRONG>. <BR><BR><STRONG>Bronfman</STRONG>, heir to the Seagram whisky fortune, was backed in the bid by venture- capital companies including <STRONG>Thomas H Lee Partners</STRONG> and <STRONG>Bain Capital</STRONG>. <STRONG>The group has been linked to a possible merger with EMI but a flotation is now believed to be its preferred first option.</STRONG> <BR><BR><STRONG>Music-industry executives have named Britain’s Sanctuary as a possible target but sources close to both sides say no talks have taken place. Another potential target is Hollywood talent agency The Firm.</STRONG> <BR><BR><STRONG>Thomas H Lee and Bain recently took a stake in The Firm whose clients include Limp Bizkit, the Dixie Chicks and Robert De Niro.</STRONG> <BR><BR>Since the takeover <STRONG>Bronfman</STRONG> has cut $250m in costs and axed over 1,000 jobs. <STRONG>Warner</STRONG>’s labels have been consolidated and 25% of acts dropped. Analysts reckon the group could now be worth in excess of $3 billion. </P>
<P>Source: <STRONG>The Times</STRONG>.</P>
</font></div>
